The scarcity of large-scale relevance judgments bottlenecks scientific literature retrieval. Existing Weak Supervision (WS) methods aggregate noisy heuristics under the assumption that each labelling function's (LF's) reliability is globally constant across the corpus, ignoring how this reliability varies with a document's position in the citation topology. This proposed research investigates a structure-aware WS paradigm built on the hypothesis of Topological Homophily of Heuristic Reliability, and its implications for identifiability and efficient online inference.